[Music]  [Music]  my name is Alice Holman and I play tenor  smith-corona with the Boston typewriter  Orchestra the Boston typer - orchestra  is an ensemble for typewriter invoice  some of us have musical backgrounds some  of us don't several of our members play  in bands currently I haven't touched  much of anything except for typewriters  since playing the trombone in high  school we play a wide variety of  typewriters from some small portable  models all the way up to some relatively  large desk models a lot of what we do in  building up songs is really exploring  what different sounds we can get out of  the typewriter and how we can  incorporate them into a song for example  you have the the key makes it of F a  basic clack sound but then you have the  the shift that moves a lot of mechanics  and make somewhat of a bass sound then  you also have the the roller that gets a  get sort of a mechanical ratchet sound  to it and then all of them we can work  with the the carriage return for like  that you know the standard sweep e sound  to it as our name suggests the Boston  typewriter Orchestra is Boston based but  we've actually played all over New  England  most of the typewriters are 50 60 70  years old usually in our hands we see  two to three years of lifespan we feed  on them pretty hard when we started out  in 2004 we were we were a bunch of guys  getting together and kind of banging on  typewriters we get that we're kind of  weird it's a little bit of a wacky idea  but we've discovered that this makes  some legitimate music and you know it  can be a real serious endeavor we have  true songs that come together and people  seem to really enjoy them and as long as  we still are entertaining people we're  gonna stay out there typing  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  very often people think it's gonna be  funny and then they realize it's not  really we really do music it's not  impossible to make music with vegetables  [Music]  my name is Susanna got Naya and then I'm  a member of the vegetable orchestra the  orchestra exists since 18 years now if  there's instrument roasted tomato  because you can do sound with tomatoes  but it will be messy for sure we go  shopping on the market first we choose  all the vegetables there we have three  kinds of instruments one is the ready  mate you can just buy it at the market  and play it it's like the pepper it's  cold it's ready already and then there  are the simple instruments like with one  cut or some cuts to get it like that  then with the more complex instruments  we have some that work like normal  instruments you know the pumpkin is the  bass drum and the sound of the pumpkin  is really important after shopping we go  to the venue and start to build  instruments all in all it takes 2 to 3  hours to build all the instruments for  everybody and then we start with the  soundcheck and since we have new  instruments each time we have to have a  very long soundcheck  [Music]  [Applause]  we have a lot of different musical  influences in the orchestra since  they're really many people everybody's  interested in different things we make  the soup during the building process of  the vegetable instruments with the  remaining vegetables when we serve it to  the audience after the concert and it's  also after hearing and seeing us and  smelling the vegetables which will be  intense in there because it's so small  this theater and then you can also eat  it you never get it out of your head  anymore if you're in the orchestra to  look vegetables in another way every  place in the world sounds unique these  sound scapes tell us a story about that  place they tell us the ways in which  global warming is beginning to change  the natural soundscape  [Music]  just before the Sun rises at first light  is the dawn chorus the birds begin to  sing and the insects and the amphibians  and that's the time of day to go out and  listen I'm a soundscape  ecologist that record animals for a  living  this is Sugarloaf State Park every  spring I can probably be found there  once a week recording in the same spot  using the same kind of equipment so that  I can repeat these recordings over and  over again and compare them and I have  quite a archive of material which shows  over time this habitat has been changing  pretty radically there's a result of  global warming and the California  drought the first recording made in 2004  shows a very robust habitat with a  signature of a stream in 2014 things  were changing radically it was already  the second year of a major drought in  California and in 2015 we had what I  call a Silent Spring over 50% of my  collection comes from habitats that are  so radically altered they're either  altogether silent or no longer be heard  in their original form here's an example  from Costa Rica  [Music]  that's before logging this is after  same spot this is a coral reef the new  allez-vous and Fiji part of it is dying  and part of it is still living here's  what the living part sounds like here's  what the dying part sounds like hardly  any fish sounds  we really don't know what this means in  the long run in the short run were  beginning to lose these soundscapes  because habitats are just changing so  radically and we're partly to blame for  them when I first began to record almost  fifty years ago I would go out into the  field and just put on a pair of  earphones and sit and listen because it  made me feel good it made me aware of  the living world around me and so I want  to do anything that does that for me  [Music]  I'm sure someone were to look inside my  window while I'm playing they probably  would have no idea what's going on in  here to watch a theremin being played  looks really strange my name is Pamela  and Stickney and one of the instruments  I'd love to play is severa man like what  the hell was that in fact I don't know  so the theremin is a synthesizer and  it's a synthesizer that's not controlled  with the use of a keyboard like a piano  I'm controlling the pitches with my left  hand and this hand is just the  articulation hands the volume  [Music]  it's controlled without any physical  contact and you can make it like a birds  [Music]  the really big challenge with the  instrument is its reacting not just to  my hands and how they're moving in my  fingers it's sensitive as well into my  whole body I've recorded on an album of  David Burns Grace Jones the Indigo Girls  it's such a new instrument still that  there's nobody who's going to stand  there and say you're not doing it the  way it's supposed to be done I don't  think there's anything else like it it  doesn't relate to anything else I've  ever played  [Music]  I'm the first woman to graduate with a  doctorate from Juilliard and now I play  the toy piano life works in mysterious  ways  I have transformed the toy piano into a  rail instrument to play at the Toy Barn  oh well you have to practice on it every  bit as hard as you would on a normal  piano in fact I think you have to work  even harder because the action is so  primitive you can make it sound  beautiful you can make it sound  convincing it's a bit like going down  the rabbit hole and taking your audience  with you way back down this Avenue to  your childhood days what a skeptical  person comes to a toy piano concert that  I give I'm able to turn him around it's  a whole extended family now of people  into toy pianos  it's a fearless little band and we're  willing to stick their necks out for  toys  [Music]  [Music]

